  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nOur dry weather continues today, as it will through the remainder\nof the forecast period. High pressure is maintaining steady\ncontrol across the eastern half of the CONUS. PWs will remain\nbelow 3/4 inch over the next few days, then as low level flow\nshifts more easterly, the airmass will be reinforced by drier and\nsomewhat cooler air, with PW falling to 1/4-1/2 inch (around 50\npct of normal) in most areas. Within this dry regime, diurnal \ntemperature ranges will be greater, with cool mornings and warm \nafternoons. With the shift to easterly flow, high temps won't be \nquite as warm over the weekend into Monday, but they will still \nbe near to slightly above normal.\n\nGiven the persistent dry pattern, fire weather will remain the \nprimarily concern over the upcoming week. Antecedent ground \nconditions/fuels are exceptionally dry (daily max ERCs in the \n90th+ percentile) with RHs falling to 20-30 percent each afternoon\nand at times falling into the teens. However, winds are forecast\nto remain relatively light throughout this time frame, which is\nwhy we don't imminently anticipate a more critical situation \nworthy of red flag products. Regardless, great caution is still\nwarranted with any outdoor burning over the next several days, or\nit should be avoided altogether, as local burn bans continue to \nbe added across our area. If there are any signs in later \nforecasts of stronger wind, messaging may need to be elevated \naccordingly. /DL/",
